# DUP(2)

## NAME

dup, dup2 - duplicate an open file descriptor

## SYNOPSIS

```c
int dup(int fildes);

int dup2(int fildes, int fildes2);
```

## DESCRIPTION

Given a file descriptor returned from an *open, pipe,* or *creat* call, *dup* allocates another
file descriptor synonymous with the original. The new file descriptor is returned.

In the second form of the call, *fildes* is a file descriptor referring to an open file, and
*fildes2* is a non-negative integer less than the maximum value allowed for file descriptors
(approximately 19). *Dup2* causes *fildes2* to refer to the same file as *fildes.* If *fildes2*
already referred to an open file, it is closed first.

## SEE ALSO

creat(2), open(2), close(2), pipe(2)

## DIAGNOSTICS

The value -1 is returned if: the given file descriptor is invalid; there are already too many
open files.

## BESM-6 NOTES

**This is a two-argument gate whichever name you call,** and that is why its stub is
hand-written rather than generated.

A C **dup(fd)** has *one* argument, so the compiler leaves *fd* in the accumulator and pushes
nothing. The gate expects two: it would read its first from an untouched word below r15 and
then *pop that word,* drifting the caller's stack by one on every call. The stub pushes the
missing word itself.

*Dup2* is the same gate with bit 0100 set in the first argument, exactly as v7 does it - there
is no second syscall number.

## ASSEMBLER

**Note:** v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here - a decimal call number and a `sys name; arg ...`
line. Neither is how a call is made on this machine. The gate is one extracode - **$77
SYS_dup** - and the C calling convention has already put the arguments where it reads them:
1..n-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator, the error number
in r14.

**The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.** The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is
the only place a syscall number appears on the user side. See intro(2) for the whole contract.
